# Passkeys, recovery codes and the authenticator app

Nocturne has no passwords. This page walks through what signing in actually looks like, and
the two fallbacks that keep an account reachable when the usual device isn't to hand.

## Signing in with a passkey

A **passkey** is a credential kept on your phone, laptop, tablet or hardware security key
and unlocked the way you already unlock that device — a fingerprint, a face scan, or its
PIN. Nothing secret is typed in, and nothing secret is stored on the instance, so there is no
password to be guessed, reused or leaked.

There are two ways in, both on the sign-in page:

- **Sign in with passkey** — the main button. Your device offers the passkeys it holds for
  this instance and you pick one. You don't need to type a username.
- **Sign in with username** — you type your username first, then unlock the passkey
  registered to that account. Use this when the first button doesn't offer the account you
  wanted, which usually means the passkey lives on a different device.

<Callout type="info" title="Browser support">
  Passkeys need a browser that supports them. On a browser that doesn't, the sign-in screen
  says so and points you at a recovery code or a different browser instead, so an account is
  never stranded.
</Callout>

## The authenticator app step

An **authenticator app** — Aegis, 1Password, Google Authenticator and others — shows a
six-digit code that changes every thirty seconds. The technical name for this is **TOTP**,
short for time-based one-time password.

It is optional, and it is a **second step rather than a way in on its own**. Once you have
enrolled an authenticator app, signing in with your passkey is followed by a prompt for the
current six-digit code. Both have to succeed.

<Callout type="info" title="It applies to passkey sign-ins">
  The authenticator step follows a passkey. If you sign in through a social login provider
  instead, that provider handles any extra steps of its own, and Nocturne does not ask again.
</Callout>

### Setting one up

Go to **Settings > Account** and choose the authenticator app option. Nocturne shows a QR
code; scan it with your authenticator app, then type the six-digit code the app produces to
confirm the two are in step:

<Screenshot id="totp-setup" />

Scan the code with the app before leaving the page — it isn't shown again afterwards.

### Signing in afterwards

From then on, sign in as usual with your passkey and a second screen asks for the current
code from your app. Open the app, read the six digits, type them in.

<Screenshot id="totp-challenge" />

<Callout type="warning" title="Keep a way back in">
  If you enrol an authenticator app, make sure you still have your recovery codes. Losing
  the phone that holds both your passkey and your authenticator app, with no recovery code
  saved, means losing the account.
</Callout>

## Recovery codes

**Recovery codes** are a short list of one-time codes issued when your account is set up.
Each one works once and then is spent. They exist for the day the usual device isn't
available — lost, broken, or simply left at home.

To use one, follow the **Use a recovery code** link on the sign-in page and enter
a code you haven't used yet.

Store them somewhere you can reach without the device you normally sign in with: a password
manager on another device, or printed and kept somewhere safe. A copy saved only on the
phone that holds your passkey is no help when that phone is the thing you've lost.

## If you're locked out

Work through, in order:

1. **Another device.** A passkey saved in a password manager or synced through your phone
   or laptop account is often already on your other devices.
2. **A recovery code.** Any unused code gets you in, and once in you can register a fresh
   passkey on the device you're holding.
3. **The instance owner.** On an instance someone else runs, the owner can remove and re-invite your
   member account. See [Member accounts and invites](/docs/sharing/members).
